.TH INPUTTEST 1
.SH NAME
inputtest \- log the input the system received
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B inputtest
[
.B -w
]
[
.B -p
]
[
.B -o
.I logfile
]
[
.B -n
.I events
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Inputtest
reads
.B /dev/keyboard
\(em or
.B /dev/pointer
with
.B -p
\(em and writes what arrives, with times, to
.I logfile
.RB ( /inputtest.log
by default).
.B -n
stops after a number of events.
.PP
It exists so that a recording made by
.B INFERNO_INPUT_RECORD
can be checked against something read inside the process rather than against a
screenshot.
A window that looks right proves much less than a log of exactly which keys
arrived and when, and it cannot be compared automatically between a recorded
run and a replayed one.
.PP
Keys are logged as numbers, not characters.
Key
.I releases
arrive here as well as presses, as codes above 59000, and a log of characters
would not show them \(em which is exactly how a Tk bug that inserted releases
as text stayed invisible to typing into a window and looking at it.
.SS \fB-w\fP, and why a recording can be empty
.B -w
allocates a display before reading.
Without a window on screen the host window driver has no key window and never
calls
.BR gkbdputc ,
so nothing is delivered and the recording is empty for a reason that has
nothing to do with the recorder.
This cost real time: the first recordings made here were 16 bytes, a header and
no events.
.SH EXAMPLE
Record a session, then run it again with no hands:
.IP
.EX
INFERNO_INPUT_RECORD=/rec.in emu -r . /dis/sh.dis \e
	-c 'inputtest -w -o /a.log -n 9'	# and type
INFERNO_INPUT_REPLAY=/rec.in emu -r . /dis/sh.dis \e
	-c 'inputtest -w -o /b.log -n 9'	# and do not
.EE
.PP
Typing
.B hello
gives the same nine events in the same order at the same times, to within a
millisecond or two:
.IP
.EX
recorded   0 104 | 11 59496 | 11 101 | 23 59493 | 23 108 ...
replayed   0 104 |  6 59496 |  6 101 | 21 59493 | 21 108 ...
.EE
.PP
With
.BR -p ,
the distinct pointer states come back identical, and so do the intervals
between the times the device reports:
.B (252,290,0)@7019
,
.B (277,305,0)@7204
,
.BR (302,320,0)@7390 .
.PP
It is the intervals that matter and the intervals that are guaranteed.
Consumers compare differences of that msec to detect a double click, so it must
be carried through rather than restamped on injection.
The absolute values matched here as well, because a replay's clock starts when
.B emu
does, but nothing promises that and nothing should depend on it.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/inputtest.b
