.SH BUGS
It reads one device at a time.
.PP
.B /dev/pointer
reports the current state rather than a queue of events, so a reader faster
than the events repeats samples, and the number of repeats differs between two
runs without anything being wrong.
Compare distinct consecutive states, not lines.
.PP
Button presses were not exercised end to end here.
Synthetic clicks from the host are unreliable on macOS and none arrived, so
what has been shown for buttons is only that they travel in the same record,
by the same code, as the position and time that were verified.
.PP
Nothing here reproduces
.I execution.
Dis scheduling is not recorded, so a program racing two of its own processes
can still diverge on identical input.
